ode:\n\nimport math\n\ndef solve(case):\n    matrix = case['matrix']\n    # validate nonempty rectangular matrix\n    if not matrix or not matrix[0]:\n        raise ValueError(\"empty matrix\")\n    n_rows = len(matrix)\n    n_cols = len(matrix[0])\n    # ensure all rows have same length\n    for row in matrix:\n        if len(row) != n_cols:\n            raise ValueError(\"non-rectangular matrix\")\n    # compute document frequency per column (feature)\n    df = [0] * n_cols\n    for row in matrix:\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val != 0:\n                df[j] += 1\n    # compute idf\n    idf = [math.log(1 + n_rows / (1 + d)) for d in df]\n    # compute weighted matrix and row degrees\n    weighted_matrix = []\n    row_degree = []\n    for row in matrix:\n        weighted_row = [val * idf[j] for j, val in enumerate(row)]\n        weighted_matrix.append(weighted_row)\n        row_degree.append(sum(weighted_row))\n    # round\n    idf_rounded = [round(x, 4) for x in idf]\n    weighted_matrix_rounded = [[round(x, 4) for x in row] for row in weighted_matrix]\n    row_degree_rounded = [round(x, 4) for x in row_degree]\n    return {\n        \"idf\": idf_rounded,\n        \"row_degree\": row_degree_rounded,\n        \"weighted_matrix\": weighted_matrix_rounded\n    }\n\nBut note: rounding may produce -0.0, which is fine. Also, round(0.0, 4) = 0.0.\n\nNow, I need to produce the full response as a JSON object with single key \"implementation\". The value is a string of Python source. I must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes within that string. Use JSON serialization.\n\nI'll write the code in a single string with triple quotes for readability, then escape for JSON.\n\nI'll use json.dumps to create the JSON string.\n\nBut the entire response must be a JSON object. So my response should be: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import math\\n    ...\"}\n\nI'll construct that.\n\nMake sure to not include markdown. Output exactly one JSON object.\n\nNow, before finalizing, check if there are any additional requirements: \"Do not access files, the network, environment variables, subprocesses, randomness, or clocks.\" That's fine.\n\nAlso \"must not mutate case\": we are not mutating.\n\nAlso \"The evaluator invokes solve(case) once for each fixture row. The argument is exactly the inner object stored in that row's payload field.\" So case will be the payload dictionary. So we access case['matrix'].\n\nAlso note: In the fixture examples, each payload has \"matrix\" and \"_case_nonce\". We ignore _case_nonce.\n\nNow, the rounding: maybe the expected output uses more precision? The candidate_artifact says \"Round\" but not how many decimals. Could be to 4 decimal places as common in such tasks. But let's see if there's any hint from the task name \"DATA-SNAP-01\". I'll assume 4 decimal places.\n\nAlternatively, we could not round and rely on the evaluator to compare with tolerance. But the instruction says \"Round and return\", so I must round. I'll stick with 4 decimal places.\n\nNow, produce the JSON response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1302,"completion_tokens":2530,"total_tokens":3832,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2191},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1280,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":22},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}